  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nREST OF TODAY/TONIGHT: Sprinkles/showers continue to move east this \nafternoon, mainly along and north of I-90. First round of showers \nwith the initial WAA and the LLJ continues to push east of I-29 this \nafternoon. There's been enough MUCAPE (generally 500 J/kg or less) \nfor some lightning near and north of US Hwy 14, but this risk has \nquickly diminished with the loss of the instability. No lightning \nhas been observed in our area for the last hour or so, and think \nrisk is now < 10% for the rest of the afternoon. Next area of \nshowers across central and northeastern SD is tied to the mid level \nwave and moves through during the afternoon and early evening hours, \nagain staying mainly north of I-90. With dry sub cloud layer and \nhigh cloud bases, any rainfall accumulation remains minimal today. \n\nStrongest winds have diminished, so allowed the Wind Advisory to \nexpire at 1 PM CDT. Expect southerly gusts ahead of the front 30-40 \nmph this afternoon. Have also observed some 30-40 mph gusts from the \nnorthwest just behind the front, which will continue into the \nevening hours. Front has been moving through a little more quickly \nthan previously expected. Despite showers and breezy conditions, \nit's a pleasant early October day with temps in the 60s and 70s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to slide in from the northwest late \ntonight. With winds tapering down overnight and clearing skies, \nwould not be entirely surprised to see some patchy river valley fog \nby daybreak Sunday. HREF probability of visibility below 5 miles is \nlow (less than 30%) and very isolated, so kept mention out of the \nforecast for now. We'll fall into the mid 40s and lower 50s. \n\nSUNDAY: Surface high pressure remains overhead to end the weekend, \nwith neutral to CAA and light northwesterly winds most of the day. \nThis should keep temperatures a bit cooler for most of the area, \nwith highs in the mid and upper 60s. Lows fall into the mid and \nupper 40s again as surface high moves east. \n\nTHE UPCOMING WEEK: Mid/upper level high pressure remains in place \nthrough the work week across the southwestern US. This keeps the \ncentral and northern Plains in northwesterly flow, allowing a few \nshort waves to track across the region. Highs in the 60s and 70s \nwith lows generally in the 40s and 50s. \n\nMonday night into Tuesday sees a couple of these subtle and weak \nwaves through, as well as a cold front Tuesday. Soundings for this \nperiod are very dry, so precipitation is not expected. Main impact \nwill be breezier winds Monday afternoon and night with gusts 30-40 \nmph as the SPG tightens ahead of the front. Tuesday looks to be the \nwarmest day of the upcoming week, although how warm depends on how \nquickly the front tracks through. \n\nGuidance is quite varied handling the next surface low Thursday and \nany associated rain chances. Deterministic GFS is currently the \nstrongest with this feature and would bring more widespread \nprecipitation chances; however, confidence is low given model spread \nand ensemble probability of more than a tenth of an inch less than \n20%, so have left the very low NBM pops as is. Troughing begins to \ndig into the western US by next weekend bringing the return of \nsouthwesterly flow and possibly more precipitation chances.",
